<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Amir Khusrau</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1253–1325</span> · Also known as Amir Khusro</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amir">Amir</a> Khusrau was a poet and musician of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a> who served several sultans and was a disciple of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> saint <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizamuddin-auliya">Nizamuddin Auliya</a>. He wrote mainly in Persian, including historical poems on the Khalji sultans, but also verse in Hindavi. Called the 'Parrot of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>', he is credited with shaping qawwali and North Indian music.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/amir-khusrau.jpg" alt="Amir Khusrau" width="320" height="246" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Anonymous artist from Bokhara · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Amir_Khusro.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
